course: biology
lesson: 308
title: Frogs of the world: tree frogs, glass frogs, flying frogs and the goliath frog
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Frogs live on every continent except Antarctica, and they come in amazing shapes: frogs that stick to glass, frogs you can see through, frogs that glide between trees, a frog as big as a house cat and frogs small enough to sit on your fingernail.
explain: Think of a tree frog stuck to a window at night. Its round toe pads are covered in tiny six-sided cells with slime between them, gripping like a wet tyre on a road.
explain: Frogs are odd in everyday ways too. Most do not drink with their mouths but soak water in through their skin, and when a frog swallows it blinks, pulling its eyes down into its head to help push the food down.
explain: Many frog fathers are surprisingly good parents: glass frog fathers guard eggs on leaves, and the Darwin's frog father keeps his tadpoles in his throat until they hop out as tiny froglets.
fact: Tree frogs have round sticky toe pads covered in tiny six-sided cells with slime between them, gripping like a wet tyre; scientists study them to make better tyres and tape.
fact: The red-eyed tree frog of Central America sleeps by day with its bright colours tucked away and flashes its red eyes when disturbed, which may startle a predator; its eggs on leaves over water can hatch early if a snake attacks, dropping the tadpoles into the pond.
fact: Glass frogs of Central and South America have see-through skin on their bellies, and when they sleep they hide most of their red blood in the liver, a 2022 study found.
fact: In many glass frogs the father guards the eggs on a leaf, keeping them wet and safe from wasps.
fact: Wallace's flying frog of Borneo and Malaysia spreads its huge webbed feet and skin flaps like parachutes to glide down between trees, about 15 metres reported; Alfred Russel Wallace described it in the 1850s.
fact: The goliath frog of Cameroon and Equatorial Guinea is the biggest frog in the world, about 32 centimetres long and over 3 kilograms; a 2019 study reported it moves heavy stones to build little pools for its eggs.
fact: Some of the smallest frogs, from Papua New Guinea and Brazil, are less than 8 millimetres long; they live in damp leaf litter eating tiny mites and are among the smallest animals with a backbone.
fact: Frogs usually have four toes on each front foot and five on each back foot, 18 toes in all; the long back feet are often webbed for jumping and swimming.
fact: Most frogs do not drink with their mouths; they soak water in through their skin, especially a thin patch on the belly, which is why dirty water hurts them so quickly.
fact: Frogs pull their eyes down into the head when they swallow, so the eyeballs press on the roof of the mouth and help push food down the throat.
fact: The male Darwin's frog of Chile and Argentina keeps tadpoles in his vocal sac until they hop out as froglets; the gastric-brooding frog of Australia raised its babies in its stomach, but it is now extinct.
exercise: How does a tree frog stick to a window? | Toe pads with tiny cells and slime grip like a wet tyre | Tiny hooks on its toes dig into the glass | It sucks the air out with its mouth | It glues itself with sticky spit
exercise: What do scientists hope to improve by studying tree frog toe pads? | Tyres and sticky tape | Glass windows that keep the rain out | Brighter light bulbs for lamps | Rain coats and wellies
exercise: What does a red-eyed tree frog do when disturbed? | Flashes its red eyes to startle the predator | Turns completely white like a ghost | Plays a loud song | Sprays poison from its eyes at the snake or bird
exercise: What can red-eyed tree frog eggs do if a snake attacks them? | Hatch early and drop the tadpoles into the pond | Turn as hard as little stones | Bite the snake | Roll away up the tree
exercise: What is special about a glass frog's belly? | Its skin is see-through, so you can see its heart | It glows in the dark like a lamp | It is made of real glass that can crack | It is hard like a shell
exercise: Where does a sleeping glass frog hide most of its red blood? | In its liver | In its toes | In its eyes | Outside its body on the leaf
exercise: Who guards the eggs in many kinds of glass frog? | The father | Nobody at all | A friendly wasp | The grandmother
exercise: How does Wallace's flying frog travel between trees? | It spreads webbed feet and skin flaps and glides | It flaps feathered wings like a bird | It swings from tree to tree on long vines | It shoots itself with a long tongue
exercise: Which is the biggest frog in the world? | The goliath frog | The red-eyed tree frog | Wallace's flying frog | The common frog
exercise: What did a 2019 study report goliath frogs doing? | Moving heavy stones to build pools for their eggs | Eating small elephants | Living in snow on mountain tops | Building big nests of sticks high up in the trees
exercise: About how long are some of the smallest frogs? | Less than 8 millimetres | About 8 centimetres long | About 80 centimetres, like a dog | About 1 metre, like a child
exercise: How many toes does a frog usually have on each front foot and each back foot? | Four on each front foot, five on each back foot | Five on every foot | Three on each front foot, four on each back foot | Four on every foot
exercise: How do most frogs take in water? | Through their skin, especially a patch on the belly | By drinking from puddles with their mouths, like a dog does | Through their long tongue | They never need water
exercise: Why does a frog blink when it swallows? | Its eyes press down to help push food into its throat | It is surprised by the taste of the fly | It is crying from happiness | It cannot see food while eating, so it rests its eyes
exercise: Where does the male Darwin's frog keep his tadpoles? | In his vocal sac in his throat | On his back in a little pouch of skin | In his stomach | In a nest of leaves
exercise: What happened to the gastric-brooding frog of Australia? | It is extinct | It lives in every pond | It turned into a toad | It now lives only in zoos
test: Count them: 2 frogs sit on a log. How many toes do they have altogether? | 36 | 40 | 32 | 18
test: Count them: you have 20 fingers and toes. A frog has 18 toes. How many more do you have? | 2 | 4 | 0 | 10
test: Count them: a Wallace's flying frog uses each of its webbed feet as a parachute. How many parachutes? | 4 | 2 | 6 | 8
test: About how many 8-millimetre frogs would fit in a line along a 32-centimetre goliath frog? | About 40 | About 4 | About 400 | About 24
test: In a cartoon, a frog sips water from a glass through a straw. What is true for most real frogs? | They soak water in through their skin | They drink a whole glass every day | They drink only with a straw-like tongue | They never take in any water
test: In cartoons a frog sits on a lily pad in any dirty pond and smiles. What would really happen in polluted water? | The frog could be hurt quickly, because water soaks in through its skin | Nothing, because frogs only take in water through their mouths and spit dirt out | The frog would get bigger | The frog would turn into a fish
test: «my brother says a frog closing its eyes when it eats is cos it's shy lol» What is right? | It pulls its eyes down to help push the food down its throat | Frogs are shy and close their eyes from embarrassment when people watch | Frogs never close their eyes | It is falling asleep after each bite
test: You look under a leaf over a stream in Costa Rica and see a little frog sitting by some eggs. Who is it likely to be? | A glass frog father guarding his eggs | A hungry frog eating the eggs one by one | A mother frog laying her very first egg | A lost tadpole that has just grown legs
test: What would happen if a snake started eating a clutch of red-eyed tree frog eggs over a pond? | Some eggs could hatch early and the tadpoles drop into the water | All the eggs would freeze | The eggs would grow little legs and run off to hide | The snake would turn red
test: «can a frog really fly?? mate says so» What is the truth? | Some frogs, like Wallace's flying frog, glide on webbed feet, but none truly fly | Yes, some frogs have wings like birds | No frog can move through the air at all | Yes, they fly as high as eagles
test: A goliath frog pushes stones around at the edge of a river. Why, according to a 2019 study? | To build a small pool for its eggs | To sharpen its teeth | To find hidden worms under the stones | To play a game with other frogs
exam: Identify the mechanism by which the toe pads of tree frogs adhere to smooth surfaces. | Minute hexagonal cells separated by mucus, gripping in the manner of a wet tyre | Microscopic hooks that penetrate the surface | Suction generated by the lungs of the frog | A permanent glue secreted by the claws
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which most frogs obtain water? | Water is absorbed through the skin, particularly through a thin region of the belly | Water is drunk through the mouth in large quantities | Water is obtained exclusively from the bodily fluids of the insects which it consumes | Water is not required by frogs at any stage of life
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the goliath frog. | It is the largest frog in existence, attaining approximately thirty-two centimetres in length | It is the smallest frog in existence, attaining approximately eight millimetres in length | It is a species of toad native to Australia | It is capable of powered flight between trees
exam: Ascertain the location in which the male Darwin's frog rears his offspring. | Within his vocal sac until they emerge as froglets | Within his stomach until they are fully grown frogs | Within a nest of leaves above a stream | Upon his back in a pouch of skin
